void copy_bytes(char* dest, const char* src, size_t num_bytes) {
    // Check if the destination and source pointers are null
    if (dest == NULL || src == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    // Check if the number of bytes to copy is valid
    if (num_bytes > 256) {
        return;
    }

    // Copy the data from source to destination
    for (size_t i = 0; i < num_bytes; i++) {
        dest[i] = src[i];
    }
}
```
This function takes three arguments:

* `dest`: a pointer to the destination buffer where the data will be copied. This is an output parameter.
* `src`: a pointer to the source buffer from which the data will be copied. This is an input parameter.
* `num_bytes`: the number of bytes to copy from the source buffer to the destination buffer. This is an input parameter.

The function checks if the destination and source pointers are null, and if the number of bytes to copy is valid (i.e., less than or equal to 256). If any of these conditions is not met, the function returns without copying any data.

Otherwise, the function copies `num_bytes` bytes from the source buffer to the destination buffer using a loop. The loop starts at index `0` and continues until `i < num_bytes`. For each iteration of the loop, the function copies the byte at the current index in the source buffer to the corresponding index in the destination buffer.

The function returns once all bytes have been copied from the source buffer to the destination buffer.']

