Here is a sample C code that reads a file from a safe directory:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define SAFE_DIRECTORY "/safe"

int main() {
    char filename[256];
    printf("Enter the name of the file to read: ");
    scanf("%s", filename);

    // Check if the file exists in the safe directory
    if (access(SAFE_DIRECTORY, R_OK) == -1) {
        perror("File does not exist");
        return 1;
    }

    // Read the file content
    FILE *fp = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file\n");
        return 1;
    }

    char buffer[256];
    while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), fp)) {
        printf("%s", buffer);
    }

    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define a macro `SAFE_DIRECTORY` which contains the path to the safe directory. We then prompt the user for the name of the file to read and store it in a variable called `filename`.

Next, we check if the file exists in the safe directory using the `access()` function. If the file does not exist or the user does not have the necessary permissions to access the file, we print an error message and return 1.

If the file exists, we open it for reading using the `fopen()` function. If the file cannot be opened, we print an error message and return 1.

Finally, we read the contents of the file line by line using a loop that reads each line into a buffer until the end of the file is reached. We then print each line to the console.

Note that this code assumes that the safe directory is located at `/safe` and that the user has permission to access the file. If your application requires additional functionality or different paths, you may need to modify the code accordingly.']

