    if (status == 0) {
        printf("The command was successful.\n");
    } else {
        fprintf(stderr, "The command failed with exit code %d\n", status);
    }

    return 0;
}

int run_command(const char* command, const char* arg) {
    pid_t pid;
    int status;

    if ((pid = fork()) < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Fork failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        return -1;
    } else if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process
        execlp("id", "id", arg, NULL);
        fprintf(stderr, "Execlp failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    } else {
        // Parent process
        waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
        return WEXITSTATUS(status);
    }
}
```
This code uses the `fork()` and `execlp()` functions to run the `id` command with the specified username as an argument. The `waitpid()` function is used to wait for the child process to finish and get its exit status. If the exit status is 0, it means that the command was successful, otherwise it means that it failed with a non-zero exit code.

The `run_command` function takes two arguments: the name of the command to run (`id` in this case) and the username as an argument. It forks a child process and executes the `id` command with the specified username as an argument using the `execlp()` function. If an error occurs during the fork or exec, it is handled by printing an error message to standard error and returning -1. Otherwise, it waits for the child process to finish and returns its exit status.

